 Playing a tourist was most definitely more challenging than any role
I=92ve tried yet so far.  The lack of any starting armor but a Hawaian
shirt and the subsequent lack of any kind of magic cancellation are
very challenging, indeed. The blessed +2 darts and expensive camera
are great, but after the darts have disappeared and the camera=92s
charges are gone, without a decent weapon, you=92re toast.

 In any case, in this game, I found some crude daggers, a splint mail
and a +4 pair of hard shoes by level 5, and so was fairly well set.
Sokoban was the first real challenge. On the final level (amulet of
reflection), I=92d already used up my wand of magic missile and so had
to take out the first giant mimic by throwing any available weapon at
it =96 curved swords, rocks, knives, etc. The second mimic proved more
troublesome. Forgetting that I=92d just upped my dagger to =93skilled=94, I
threw my last dagger at it (I threw two) and didn=92t realize it. So
when I went in for the kill, figuring I could take him with my last
dagger, I discovered I was weaponless and stuck. Ack!

In desperation, I zapped it with a wand of polymorph, which
transformed itt into an orc.  I ran away, found a short sword and
dispatched it, only to find that, indeed, I=92d polymorphed all of my
daggers into a short sword as well.  Short sword it would have to be.I
eventually worked my way up to skilled before I found a unicorn horn,
but by that time, I'd maxed out my skill slots.

It=92s a good thing that I got reflection in Sokoban. As usual, I tied a
towel around my head and telepathically searched Minetown, and didn=92t
see anything that should prove troublesome. Alas, one of those gnomes
or giant rats was a chameleon and, by the time I saw it in person, it
had transformed into a black dragon. Without reflection, I think I=92d
have been a goner.

My next lucky break was finding a pair of gauntlets of force in one of
the two large armor shops in the dungeon, especially since I never
found a bag of holding, and no bag of any kind until I did the quest.
Very inconvenient, but nevertheless a great exercise to teach me
exactly what was necessary for survival, and what I typically just lug
around just because I can. I believe I truly mastered the art of the
stash .....

The dungeon was thick with temples and altars, so sacrificing was not
a problem. My first gift was the vorpal sword. Shortly afterwards,I
prayed for holy water and got crowned

I had, luckily, found a pair of cursed -2 levetation boots in the
dungeon before Medusa. After uncursing, I dispatched the snakey lady
and descended until the castle, at which point, I turned around and
headed back up to the quest.

Until the quest, I'd had no wishes, but on the final level of the
quest, I found a WoW in a shop. And, since I'd not been able to keep
any of my pets alive for any stretch of time, I paid full price. The
only decent pets I had in the game, a warhorse and a large dog, got
turned into a green slime (absolutely the very worst pet you can have)
and a newt. In fact, paying full price so often kept me from being
able to buy any more protection that 5 points -- that and the scarcity
of vaults, the non-existence of Ludios and no leprechaun halls --  but
in the end, it didn't matter, as I found three magic markers in the
dungeon, so my AC by the time I entered Gehennom was -35 .

In any case, after getting the PYEC, I burned three wishes of the WoW
(0:2) trying unsuccessfully to get Magicbane, at which point I gave up
and relied on double weaponing with the Vorpal Sword and an enchanted
silver saber. I wished up a BoH, GDSM and a ring of levitation, and
headed downward to the castle.

At the castle, I took the backdoor and cleared everything out without
problems. Beheaded Asmodeus, killed Jubilex early on after a demon
summoned him (didn't even get engulfed), stoned Orcus. The only tight
spot in Gehennom occurred on the way back up, when a bunch of monsters
surrounded my on Jubilex's swamp, included a Couatl, and I wasn't
wearing my oilskin cloak. Thank goodness for wands of teleportation.

 In the late game, I became quite the spellcaster, thanks to a robe.
And, on the way back up, in hopes of making additional holy water for
emergency uncursing and curing sickness, I sacrificed an invisible
stalker to see whether I could pray.

The Lady gave me Firebrand. Thanks.

The planes were a snap, and Rodney posed no problems either, since I'd
found three wands of death. The second altar was mine.

